            "review_comments": [
                "I couldn't figure out how to remove the `unwrap` here - if I put `.collect()` after `map`, then I get an error saying that I need a type hint, but I couldn't figure out how to do that when the result type is\r\n```\r\nimpl Iterator<Item = (IdxSize, IdxSize)> + TrustedLen + 'a\r\n```",
                "The `collect` would materialize this iterator, which is not the return type. We return a type that implements the iterator trait, so no collection. :)",
                "thanks for your review and for explaining!\r\n\r\nDo you know of another way to propagate the error from here? Because `add(tu)(&offset, *lower, tz.as_ref())` could potentially land on an ambiguous or non-existent datetime, which would raise",
                "And this could happen per element?\r\n\r\nThe iterator should then return a result.\r\n\r\nThis would be a bit of a refractor. But we might, for performance reasons, return a fallible and a non-fallible variant.",
                "yup (though it would only be fallible in the tz-aware case)\r\n\r\nOK, thanks, I'll take a look, might be a good exercise in getting better at Rust (which I'm really enjoying learning!)",
                "Yes. I'd recommend understanding the impl and dyn trait difference here. As we probably must return an enum with dynamic trait implementors here."
            ]
